{{Yearbox/in-conflicts}}
* After the cults of Elemental Evil slip dozens of ''[[devastation orb]]s'' into [[Mulmaster]], the city's harbor is destroyed when one is successfully detonated in the sewers, despite running battles between cultists and agents of the [[Five factions]] that resulted in the rest being prevented from doing significant damage.<ref>{{cite web |url=http://dndadventurersleague.org/indeed-mulmaster-is-undone/#more-3505|title=Indeed, Mulmaster is Undone |accessdate=2016-1-10 |format= |work= D&D Adventurer's League}}</ref>
* The [[mind flayer]]s [[Chishinix]], [[Hashutu]], and [[Voalsh]] work to transform Phandalin's inhabitants into mind flayers on behalf of their "god" [[Ilvaash]].<ref>{{Cite book/Phandelver and Below: The Shattered Obelisk|147}}</ref>

{{Yearbox/in-deities}}
* The returned [[Eilistraee]],<ref name="SCAG">{{Cite book/Sword Coast Adventurer's Guide|23, 108}}</ref> is seen dancing with, and speaking to, mortals up and down the Sword Coast. In particular, she appears under the walls of [[Waterdeep]], leading many of her followers there.<ref name="DM">{{Cite book/Death Masks}}</ref>
* Many deities no longer grant ''[[Resurrection (spell)|resurrection]]'' spells, except to rare exceptions.<ref name="DM">{{Cite book/Death Masks}}</ref>
* [[Mystra]] imposes heavy restrictions on the use of mind-reading and mind-reaming magic, especially when used on other spellcasters.<ref name="DM"/>

{{Yearbox/in-organizations}}
* The latest incarnation of the [[Cult of Elemental Evil]] rises as a union of four cults when the prophets, the [[moon elf]] [[Aerisi Kalinoth]], leader of the [[Cult of the Howling Hatred]]; the [[tiefling]] [[Vanifer]], leader of the [[Cult of the Eternal Flame]]; the [[medusa]] [[Marlos Urnrayle]], leader of the [[Cult of the Black Earth]]; and the [[human]] [[Gar Shatterkeel]], leader of the [[Cult of the Crushing Wave]], take possession of the elemental weapons, the [[spear]] ''[[Windvane]]'', the [[dagger]] ''[[Tinderstrike]]'', the [[war pick]] ''[[Ironfang (war pick)|Ironfang]]'', and the [[trident]] ''[[Drown]]'', form the [[Fane of the Eye]] located beneath the ruins of the ancient [[dwarf|dwarven]] fortress-city of [[Tyar-Besil]] in the [[Sumber Hills]] of the [[Dessarin Valley]] in order to summon the Princes of Elemental Evil.<ref name="PotA">{{Cite book/Princes of the Apocalypse}}</ref>
* After months of operating in secret, the Cult of the Black Earth attacks a delegation from the city of [[Mirabar]] bound for [[Waterdeep]], attracting the attention of the [[Lords' Alliance]], as well as the [[Harpers]], the [[Order of the Gauntlet]], the [[Emerald Enclave]], and even the [[Zhentarim]], who all send agents into the Dessarin Valley.<ref name="PotA"/>

{{Yearbox/in-politics}}
* Due to the actions of adventurers in [[Red Larch]], the [[Believers]], a secret cabal that runs the town, is publicly exposed, resulting in their losing control of the town and Red Larch electing a mayor.<ref name="PotA"/>

{{Yearbox/in-people}}
* [[Oreioth]], a human necromancer from a noble family in [[Baldur's Gate]], is discovered practicing his dark art in [[Lance Rock]], having taken the title "Lord of Lance Rock".<ref name="PotA"/>
* [[Jarlaxle]] acquires the [[submarine]] ''[[Scarlet Marpenoth]]'' in [[Lantan]].<ref name="WDH5e-p132">{{Cite book/Waterdeep: Dragon Heist|132}}</ref>{{Waterdeep-time}}
* The Blackstaff of [[Waterdeep]], [[Vajra Safahr]], seeks the meaning of the current year's name.<ref name="DM-p15">{{Cite book/Death Masks|15}}</ref>
* The dwarven [[Rockseeker]] brothers face difficulties after discovering the legendary mines of the [[Phandelver's Pact]] (the location of which was lost centuries ago), and they call upon adventurers to help.<ref name="LMoP-p44">{{Cite book/Dungeons & Dragons Starter Set/Lost Mine of Phandelver}}</ref>
* [[Iarno Albrek]] of the [[Lords' Alliance]] betrays his faction and becomes the leader of the [[Redbrand Ruffians]] after entering Phandalin.<ref>{{Cite book/Phandelver and Below: The Shattered Obelisk|34}}</ref>

{{Yearbox/migrations}}
* Followers of Eilistraee travel to Waterdeep after the Dark Dancer is seen dancing under the walls of the city. They seek the support of the [[Harper]] representative [[Remallia Haventree]] to create a forest-glade to Eilistraee in the Field Ward, destroyed by dragons in [[1485 DR]]. Remallia accepts their request, and the new temple is allowed.<ref name="DM"/>
* The [[Blacktongue (tribe)|Blacktongue]] tribe of [[bullywug]]s are forcibly transported to the [[Slitherswamp]] level of [[Undermountain]] by [[Halaster]].<ref name="W.DoMM-p109">{{Cite book/Waterdeep: Dungeon of the Mad Mage|109}}</ref>

{{Yearbox/deaths}}
* Aerisi Kalinoth, Marlos Urnrayle, Vanifer, and Gar Shatterkeel are killed by adventurers, destroying the Cult of Elemental Evil as their followers scatter and go into hiding.<ref name="PotA"/>
* Oreioth is killed by adventurers.<ref name="PotA"/>
* The dwarven adventurers of [[Falkir's Fist]] die within [[Muiral's Gauntlet]], just before they reached a ''[[portal]]'' to escape [[Undermountain]].<ref name="W.DoMM-p140">{{Cite book/Waterdeep: Dungeon of the Mad Mage|140}}</ref>
* [[Tharden Rockseeker]] is killed by [[Nezznar]], right after he enters [[Wave Echo Cave]].<ref name="MoP-p44">{{Cite book/Dungeons & Dragons Starter Set/Lost Mine of Phandelver|44}}</ref>

{{Yearbox/in-environment}}
* In the spring, many households in [[Baldur's Gate]] are afflicted by [[shellfish]] poisoning.<ref name="BG:DiA-p178">{{Cite book/Baldur's Gate: Descent into Avernus|178}}</ref>

{{Yearbox/misc}}
* The ancient dwarven fortress-city of [[Tyar-Besil]], stronghold of the dwarven kingdom of [[Besilmer]], is rediscovered by adventurers battling the Cult of Elemental Evil. Once the cult is beaten, dwarves slowly begin to reclaim the city.<ref name="PotA"/>
* In the spring, a pale and silent murderer dressed in ancient [[patriar]] attire begins attacking the common people of [[Baldur's Gate]]'s [[Baldur's Gate/Outer City|Outer City]]. Its appearance coincides with a visit to the city by a mysterious relative of the [[Bormul]] family, who left several trinkets in the family crypts.<ref name="BG:DiA-p178-179">{{Cite book/Baldur's Gate: Descent into Avernus|178-179}}</ref>

